we really emphasize the ways in which  strategy is an ongoing iterative process  that it is an essential part of what you  do as a leader whether you think about  what you're doing when you're doing it  as strategy or not so you're doing  strategy all the time every time you  make a decision because at the most  basic level strategy is just about  deciding how to allocate your resources  so every time you make a decision about  whether to buys inputs from one supplier  or another or whether to you know invest  in this facility or to do this marketing  plan you're doing strategy and and our  fundamental view is it's better to do  that self-consciously and to be aware  that you're doing it and to not be aware  of it and so I think we oftentimes kind  of could benefit from demystifying what  strategy is and just realize that a lot  of us and a lot of different managerial  and leadership roles are doing strategy  all the time and it's better to be  self-reflective about it than to be  doing it blindly  a strong leader is somebody who who's  almost able to think like a scientist  who's constantly able to move back and  forth between the kind of the conceptual  model or the the theory of the way they  think the world works and the reality  that they're encountering and then  they're able to translate the  experiences that we're having or that  you're having as a group into a sensible  way of thinking about the world that the  rest of the group can really get on  board with and I think so really what  leaders do is they kind of build a  community right that the responsibility  of a leader is to think about the group  as a whole in the social group that  they're operating in a manager is  usually more concerned with kind of  technical aspects of of getting the job  done so how do I build kind of the most  efficient process but people also need a  sense of belonging and they need a sense  of kind of community in a sense of  purpose and I think that's really what  the leaders goal is  you have a plan that you want to execute  and part of your job as a strategic  leader is really to turn that plan into  coherent action right and part of that  is about what investment decisions you  make and how you allocate your resources  but a lot of it is also about how you  organize your people and the way you  structure the incentives the way you  structure reporting relationships how  much attention you pay to the culture  what kind of culture that you want to  have and even at the level of things  that we think of as kind of HR like  things like recruiting and who it is  that you're going to bring onto the team  those things are all incredibly  important because if you don't have the  alignment between the way you've set up  your organization and the strategy  you're trying to accomplish and kind of  the key assumptions in that strategy  you're not going to be successful I  think the core question and strategy is  about you know when you have an  innovation or when you have an idea and  you kind of see an opportunity there the  problem that a lot of innovators run  into is that they they focus almost  entirely on the nature of the  opportunity they really think about wow  this product is really going to do  something different for people and  that's not easy to do right it is very  hard to come up with great innovations  but actually the harder part and the  thing that we really emphasize in this  course is it's not enough to come up  with the innovation you also have to  come up with the plan for how you're  going to profit from that innovation and  that is oftentimes much more difficult  and part of that involves really  recognizing what the obstacles are that  stand in your way and those can be  obstacles in terms of powerful buyers or  suppliers they can be obstacles in terms  of rivals or potential entrants into the  industry how do you build a plan how do  you build a strategy that's going to  allow you to overcome those obstacles  because often times you come to the  table with this great insight about an  innovation that's going to have value  and there's a lot of work and getting to  that point and we'd really hate to see  the the work that you've done up until  that point go to waste because you never  figured out how to actually capture the  value that you're generating  so I think you know when we think about  leadership we tend to think about  particular individuals right so we think  about Gandhi or Martin Luther King or  Steve Jobs or kind of these great  figures that we kind of all kind of  think of as role models and and I think  it's important to learn lessons from  people like that but I think it's also  important to remember that you are who  you are and that you have your own  personality that you bring to the table  and your own strengths and your own  weaknesses and that effective leaders  are ones who recognize what their  strengths are and recognize what their  weaknesses are and build their  leadership style around that and it's  really important to have alignment  between what your leadership style is  and the kind of organization that you  build and the kind of strategy that you  that you pursue I think we too much too  great of an extent think about leaders  as something that is innate I think  everybody has leadership potential and  it is something that they can develop  over time I think it could be learned by  turning to others and asking them for  kind of you know in-depth and kind of  brutally honest kind of assessments of  what what your strengths and weaknesses  are I don't think there's any doubt  though that everybody has the potential  to be a powerful leader in the right  situation and at the right time
 

